# Map And Reduce

Map and Reduce operate on ordered lists. Map preserves source order. Reduce is
a serial left fold.

Run the cells from top to bottom.

## Install The Package

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido_action, "~> 3.0.0-beta.6"}])
```

## Define Item Actions

```elixir
defmodule FlowCollections.Double do
  use Jido.Action, name: "flow_collections_double"

  @impl true
  def run(%{value: value, index: index}, _context) do
    {:ok, %{value: value * 2, index: index}}
  end
end

defmodule FlowCollections.Sum do
  use Jido.Action, name: "flow_collections_sum"

  @impl true
  def run(%{total: total, value: value}, _context) do
    {:ok, %{total: total + value}}
  end
end
```

## Map Then Reduce

```elixir
defmodule FlowCollections.Example do
  use Jido.Flow, name: "flow_collections_example"

  flow do
    map "doubled",
      collection: input(:values),
      action: FlowCollections.Double,
      params: %{
        value: item(),
        index: item_index()
      }

    reduce "total" do
      collection(result("doubled"))
      initial(%{total: 0})
      action(FlowCollections.Sum)
      params(%{
        total: accumulator(:total),
        value: item(:value)
      })
    end

    output(%{items: result("doubled"), total: result("total")})
  end
end
```

```elixir
{:ok, %{items: items, total: %{total: 12}} = result} =
  Jido.Exec.run(FlowCollections.Example, %{values: [1, 2, 3]})

[%{value: 2, index: 0}, %{value: 4, index: 1}, %{value: 6, index: 2}] = items

result
```

Map returns one ordered list of Action outputs when a scalar expression reads
its result. Map supports `item()`, `item_index()`, and `item_id()`. Reduce adds
`accumulator()`. The item ID is a runtime identifier for one collection work
unit. IDs are deterministic for the Flow, component name, and source index.
Reduce uses its own component name for item IDs, including when it reads a
Map result. Do not treat item IDs as execution IDs.

An empty Map returns an empty list. An empty Reduce returns its initial value.

A Reduce reads the complete ordered Map result without consuming it. Other
components can read the same Map result, and the Flow can return both results,
as shown above.

The Map Action runs once per input item, regardless of the number of readers.
Each Reduce runs its own serial fold over that list.

## Map Error Modes

The default `on_error: :fail_fast` stops the component on an item failure.

`on_error: :collect_errors` returns one tagged record for every item, in source
order:

```text
%{status: :ok, value: action_result}
%{status: :error, error: portable_error_map}
```

The error map uses `Jido.Flow.Error.to_map/1`: `type`, `message`, `details`,
and `retryable?`. Action failures retain their error type and existing details,
including the Map node, target, phase, item index, and item ID. A parameter
reference failure retains its reference path and reason; it does not add
target or item identity. Runtime stacktraces are not included in this map.

Choose the mode as part of author intent. A fallback or retry is not inferred.

## Concurrency

Map items can run concurrently when `max_concurrency` is greater than `1`.
The option limits the native Runic item runnables in each ready wave.

```elixir
Jido.Exec.run(
  FlowCollections.Example,
  %{values: [1, 2, 3]},
  %{},
  max_concurrency: 2
)
```

Result order stays equal to input order. Reduce work always stays serial.
Collection targets must be Actions, not Flow modules.

## Inline Collection Actions

The nested `action` form can define Map and Reduce bodies at their
point of use. Map bindings use the same `item`, `item_index`, and `item_id`
scope as Map params. Reduce also permits `accumulator`. An inline block
supplies both the Action target and params; do not add a separate params field.
Order, empty-list behavior, errors, and concurrency rules stay the same.

See the complete [inline Map and Reduce example](inline-actions.md#use-inline-actions-in-flow).
It requires `3.0.0-beta.6` or later.
